-- Seed 066: plain-language diagnosis guides for My Chart
-- Subsystem: Patient Portal. One row per condition, shown under the diagnosis wherever a
-- patient meets one -- the After-Visit Summary, the problem list, a discharge summary, an
-- ER visit. Each guide says what the condition is, why it happens, what to do, what not to
-- do, and when to get help the same day.
--
-- Idempotency: INSERT IGNORE against uq_portal_diagnosis_guide (title), so re-running
-- changes nothing and leaves wording the hospital has since edited alone.
--
-- `code` is an ICD-10 category (three characters) wherever one guide covers the whole
-- category, and an exact code where it does not. Every category used here was checked
-- against icd10_diagnoses. `keywords` carries the free-text way in, because three of the
-- four places a diagnosis is written are free text; the title is always a keyword too, so
-- it does not need repeating there.
--
-- House rules for the wording, which the staff screen repeats to whoever edits it:
--   * second person, short sentences, no jargon that is not immediately explained
--   * no medicine names and no doses -- that is the prescription's job, and it changes
--   * never "you have" or any outlook; the guide explains a condition, it does not judge
--     this patient's case
--   * nothing that could contradict the doctor: the app shows the doctor's own
--     instructions above the guide, and the guide ends by deferring to them
--   * dos / donts / when_to_seek_help are one item per line

SET NAMES utf8mb4;

INSERT IGNORE INTO `portal_diagnosis_guides`
    (`title`, `code`, `keywords`, `about`, `why_it_happens`, `common_symptoms`, `dos`, `donts`, `when_to_seek_help`, `source`, `is_active`, `created_at`)
VALUES

-- ---------------- heart, blood pressure and circulation ----------------
('High blood pressure', 'I10', 'hypertension,htn,elevated blood pressure,high bp',
 'Blood pressure is the force of blood pushing against the walls of your arteries. When it stays higher than it should over months and years, the heart has to work harder and the arteries slowly stiffen.',
 'Usually it builds up over years, from a mix of family history, weight, too much salt, too little movement, smoking, alcohol and long-running stress. Kidney and hormone problems cause it less often.',
 'Most people feel nothing at all, which is why it is usually found at a check-up. Very high readings can bring headache, dizziness, nosebleeds or blurred vision.',
 'Take the medicines you were prescribed every day, at the same time, even on days you feel completely well.\nHave your blood pressure measured regularly and write the readings down to bring to your next visit.\nCut down on salt: less instant noodles, canned goods, processed meat, bagoong, patis and soy sauce.\nAim for about 30 minutes of walking most days, building up slowly.\nKeep to a healthy weight; even a few kilos off lowers the reading.\nSleep enough, and treat heavy snoring with pauses in breathing as something to mention to your doctor.',
 'Do not stop or skip your medicine because your reading has become normal; the normal reading is the medicine working.\nDo not change your own dose, and do not take someone else''s blood pressure medicine.\nDo not add salt at the table or use salty seasoning out of habit.\nAvoid smoking, including vaping, and keep alcohol low.\nDo not rely on herbal or over-the-counter products to bring your pressure down.',
 'Chest pain or tightness, or pain spreading to the arm, neck or jaw.\nSudden weakness or numbness on one side, a drooping face, or trouble speaking or understanding.\nSudden trouble seeing, severe headache unlike your usual, or confusion.\nShortness of breath at rest, or that wakes you at night.\nA reading of 180/120 or higher, especially with any of the above.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW()),

('Coronary artery disease', 'I25', 'cad,coronary artery disease,ischemic heart disease,angina,post cabg,coronary heart disease',
 'The arteries that feed the heart muscle itself have narrowed, usually from fatty deposits built up in their walls. Narrower arteries deliver less blood, so the heart can run short of oxygen when it has to work hard.',
 'The deposits build up over decades. High blood pressure, high cholesterol, diabetes, smoking and family history all speed it up.',
 'Chest pressure, heaviness or tightness that comes on with effort or stress and eases with rest. Some people feel it mainly as breathlessness, jaw or arm discomfort, or unusual tiredness.',
 'Take every heart medicine exactly as prescribed; several of them work quietly and you will not feel them doing it.\nLearn the difference between your usual effort-related discomfort and something new or worse.\nKeep to the activity level your doctor agreed with you, and build up gradually.\nEat more vegetables, fruit and fish; grill, boil or steam rather than deep-fry.\nKeep blood pressure, cholesterol, sugar and weight under regular review.\nGo to every follow-up, including the ones where you feel fine.',
 'Do not ignore chest discomfort that is new, lasts longer, comes on at rest, or is more easily brought on than before.\nDo not smoke, and stay out of other people''s smoke.\nDo not take on sudden heavy exertion you have not worked up to.\nDo not stop your medicines on your own, and do not use anyone else''s.',
 'Chest pain or pressure lasting more than a few minutes, or coming on at rest -- treat this as an emergency.\nChest discomfort with sweating, nausea, or pain spreading to the arm, back, neck or jaw.\nSudden severe breathlessness.\nFainting, or a fast pounding heartbeat that does not settle.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW()),

('Heart failure', 'I50', 'heart failure,chf,congestive heart failure,cardiac failure',
 'The heart is not pumping as strongly or filling as easily as it should, so blood moves around the body less efficiently and fluid tends to collect in the legs, the belly and the lungs.',
 'It usually follows years of another heart problem: high blood pressure, a previous heart attack, a valve problem, an irregular rhythm, or disease of the heart muscle itself.',
 'Breathlessness on effort or when lying flat, waking at night short of breath, swollen feet and ankles, tiredness, and weight that climbs over a few days as fluid collects.',
 'Weigh yourself each morning after the toilet and before breakfast, and write it down -- a sudden climb is fluid, not fat.\nTake your water tablet and your other heart medicines exactly as prescribed.\nKeep salt low; it is salt that makes the body hold water.\nStick to the daily fluid limit your doctor set, if you were given one.\nRaise your legs when sitting, and sleep propped up if that helps you breathe.\nStay gently active within what your doctor agreed.',
 'Do not drink more fluid than you were told, including soups, halo-halo, ice and softdrinks.\nDo not skip the water tablet because going to the toilet is inconvenient.\nAvoid salty food: instant noodles, canned goods, dried fish, processed meat, bouillon cubes.\nDo not take over-the-counter painkillers of the anti-inflammatory kind without asking, as they make the body hold fluid.\nDo not ignore weight gain of 2 kg or more in two to three days.',
 'Breathlessness at rest, or that wakes you and does not settle when you sit up.\nWeight up by 2 kg or more in two to three days, or swelling climbing up the legs or into the belly.\nCoughing up frothy or pink spit.\nChest pain, fainting, or confusion.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW()),

('Irregular heartbeat (atrial fibrillation)', 'I48', 'atrial fibrillation,af,afib,a-fib,irregular heart rhythm',
 'The upper chambers of the heart beat in a fast, disorganised way instead of a steady rhythm. Because blood swirls instead of being pushed cleanly through, clots can form, and a clot that travels to the brain causes a stroke.',
 'It becomes commoner with age, and goes along with high blood pressure, heart valve problems, an overactive thyroid, heavy alcohol use, and untreated heavy snoring with pauses in breathing.',
 'A fluttering or pounding chest, an irregular pulse, breathlessness, tiredness or light-headedness. Some people notice nothing and it is found on examination.',
 'Take your blood-thinning medicine exactly as prescribed -- this is the part that prevents a stroke, and you will never feel it working.\nKeep every blood test appointment if your medicine needs monitoring.\nLearn to feel your own pulse so you can describe what it does.\nKeep alcohol low and caffeine moderate.\nHave high blood pressure and thyroid problems treated and reviewed.\nTell every doctor and dentist that you are on a blood thinner before any procedure.',
 'Do not miss doses of a blood thinner, and never stop it on your own -- the stroke risk returns quickly.\nDo not take over-the-counter painkillers, supplements or herbal products without asking, as many interact with blood thinners.\nAvoid binge drinking and energy drinks.\nDo not ignore a new fast heartbeat that does not settle.',
 'Sudden weakness or numbness on one side, a drooping face, trouble speaking, or sudden trouble seeing -- call for emergency help.\nChest pain, severe breathlessness, or fainting.\nBleeding that will not stop, blood in vomit or stool, black tarry stool, or a bad fall or head knock while on a blood thinner.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW()),

('Stroke', 'I63', 'stroke,cva,cerebrovascular accident,cerebral infarction,brain attack',
 'A stroke happens when the blood supply to part of the brain is cut off, so that area of brain is injured. What is affected afterwards depends on which part of the brain it was.',
 'Most often a clot blocks an artery feeding the brain. High blood pressure, diabetes, high cholesterol, smoking and an irregular heartbeat all make it much more likely.',
 'Sudden weakness or numbness of the face, arm or leg on one side; trouble speaking or understanding; sudden trouble seeing; sudden severe dizziness or loss of balance; sudden severe headache.',
 'Take every medicine as prescribed, especially the ones for blood pressure, cholesterol and clot prevention.\nGo to your therapy sessions and do the home exercises -- recovery continues for many months.\nHave someone help make the home safer: good lighting, clear floors, a grab bar in the bathroom.\nIf swallowing is difficult, follow exactly the food texture and thickened drinks you were advised, and sit upright to eat.\nKeep blood pressure and sugar under regular review.\nTeach the household the sudden warning signs, so nobody waits next time.',
 'Do not stop the clot-prevention or blood pressure medicine when you feel better.\nDo not smoke, and keep alcohol low.\nDo not push into exercise beyond what your therapist set, and do not walk unaided if you have been told not to.\nIf you have swallowing trouble, do not take thin liquids or solid food that was not cleared for you.',
 'Any sudden weakness, numbness, drooping face, slurred speech, or trouble seeing or walking -- call for emergency help immediately, even if it passes.\nA sudden severe headache, vomiting, a seizure, or becoming difficult to rouse.\nChoking, coughing during meals, or a fever with a new cough, which can mean food is going into the lungs.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW()),

-- ---------------- sugar, thyroid and cholesterol ----------------
('Type 2 diabetes', 'E11', 'type 2 diabetes,diabetes mellitus,dm type 2,t2dm,niddm,diabetes',
 'The sugar in your blood is higher than it should be, because the body no longer responds properly to insulin -- the hormone that moves sugar out of the blood and into the cells -- and often does not make quite enough of it either.',
 'It develops over years. Family history, extra weight around the middle, little physical activity and a diet heavy in sugar and refined starch all contribute.',
 'Many people feel well at first. As sugar climbs: passing urine often, unusual thirst, tiredness, blurred vision, weight loss without trying, itching, and cuts or infections that heal slowly.',
 'Take your medicine or insulin exactly as prescribed, and at the right time relative to meals.\nEat at regular times, fill half the plate with vegetables, and keep rice and sweet drinks to the portion you agreed with your doctor or dietitian.\nAim for about 30 minutes of walking most days.\nCheck your blood sugar as often as you were asked, and write the numbers down with the date and time.\nLook at your feet every single day -- top, sole and between the toes -- and wear closed, well-fitting footwear.\nKeep your eye, kidney, foot and dental check-ups; damage starts long before you feel it.\nCarry something sweet and tell the people around you how to spot a low sugar.',
 'Do not stop your medicine because your sugar readings have improved.\nDo not skip meals while taking diabetes medicine, as the sugar can fall too low.\nAvoid sweet drinks: softdrinks, juice, sweetened coffee and tea, and energy drinks.\nDo not walk barefoot, and do not cut your own corns or calluses or use corn-removing plasters.\nDo not use a hot water bottle or heat pad on your feet; numb skin burns without you noticing.\nDo not treat a wound at home and wait to see if it improves.\nDo not smoke -- it multiplies the damage to nerves, kidneys and arteries.',
 'Shakiness, sweating, confusion or drowsiness that does not improve after taking something sweet.\nVomiting and being unable to keep food or fluid down, or breathing that feels deep and fast with a fruity smell on the breath.\nAny foot wound, blister, redness, swelling, blackened skin, or a wound with discharge or a smell -- the same day, not next week.\nA fever or an infection that is not settling, or sugar readings that stay very high.\nSudden change in your vision.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW()),

('Type 1 diabetes', 'E10', 'type 1 diabetes,iddm,dm type 1,t1dm,juvenile diabetes',
 'The body has stopped making insulin, the hormone that moves sugar from the blood into the cells. Insulin has to be replaced every day, by injection or pump, for life.',
 'The body''s own immune system mistakenly destroyed the insulin-making cells in the pancreas. It is nobody''s fault and it was not caused by eating sugar.',
 'It usually starts quite quickly: passing a lot of urine, great thirst, weight loss, hunger, tiredness, and sometimes vomiting and deep fast breathing when sugar is very high.',
 'Take your insulin every day as prescribed; never miss a long-acting dose, even when you are not eating.\nCheck blood sugar as often as you were taught, and keep a record you can show.\nCount or estimate carbohydrate the way you were taught, and match insulin to the meal.\nCarry fast sugar with you at all times, and make sure family, school or work know what a low sugar looks like and what to do.\nLearn your sick-day rules now, before you are ill, and keep a written copy.\nWear or carry something that identifies you as having diabetes.\nKeep eye, kidney and foot checks up to date.',
 'Do not skip or delay insulin because you feel well or are not eating -- stopping it can become dangerous within hours.\nDo not reuse or share needles, and do not inject into the same exact spot every time.\nDo not exercise hard without checking your sugar and planning food or an insulin adjustment.\nDo not drink alcohol on an empty stomach.\nDo not change your own insulin plan without advice.',
 'Vomiting, belly pain, deep fast breathing, a fruity smell on the breath, or drowsiness -- this is an emergency.\nA low sugar that does not come back up after sweet food, or a fit or loss of consciousness.\nBlood sugar staying high with ketones present.\nAny illness with fever where you cannot keep fluids down.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW()),

('Diabetes with ketoacidosis', 'E11.1', 'ketoacidosis,dka,diabetic ketoacidosis',
 'Without enough insulin the body cannot use sugar for fuel, so it burns fat instead and produces acids called ketones. The blood turns acidic and the body dries out. It is a medical emergency and is treated in hospital.',
 'It is usually set off by missed insulin, an infection, another serious illness, or diabetes that has not been diagnosed yet.',
 'Great thirst and passing a lot of urine, vomiting, belly pain, deep fast breathing, a fruity or nail-polish smell on the breath, drowsiness and confusion.',
 'Once home, take your insulin exactly as prescribed, including the long-acting dose on days you eat little.\nLearn and keep your written sick-day rules: how often to check sugar and ketones, and when to call.\nCheck for ketones whenever your sugar is high or you are unwell.\nDrink water steadily when you are ill, and keep in touch with the clinic early rather than late.\nMake sure someone at home knows the warning signs.',
 'Do not ever stop insulin because you are not eating -- that is what brings this on.\nDo not try to manage vomiting with high sugar at home.\nDo not wait to see how the night goes if ketones are present.',
 'Vomiting with high blood sugar, belly pain, deep fast breathing, or a fruity smell on the breath.\nDrowsiness, confusion, or being hard to wake.\nKetones present and rising, or sugar you cannot bring down.\nAny of these means the emergency department now, not the clinic tomorrow.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW()),

('High cholesterol', 'E78', 'dyslipidemia,hyperlipidemia,high cholesterol,hypercholesterolemia,high triglycerides',
 'There is more fat circulating in your blood than there should be. Over years this fat builds into the walls of the arteries and narrows them, which is what leads to heart attacks and strokes.',
 'Partly inherited, partly diet and activity. Fried food, fatty meat, coconut-rich and palm-oil-rich cooking, sweet drinks, inactivity and extra weight all raise it; some conditions and medicines do too.',
 'None at all, in almost everyone. It is found on a blood test, which is why the test is worth doing.',
 'Take the cholesterol medicine you were prescribed daily; it works slowly and silently.\nGrill, boil, steam or bake instead of deep-frying.\nEat fish two or three times a week, and more vegetables, fruit, beans and oats.\nWalk briskly about 30 minutes most days.\nLose some weight if you are carrying extra, especially around the middle.\nHave the blood test repeated when your doctor asks, to see whether it is working.',
 'Do not stop the medicine because you feel fine -- feeling fine is the normal state of high cholesterol.\nCut down fatty and fried food: lechon, chicharon, crispy pata, fast food, fatty cuts, and heavy use of coconut milk.\nAvoid sweet drinks and limit alcohol, which both push triglycerides up.\nDo not smoke.\nDo not rely on supplements or herbal products in place of treatment.',
 'Chest pain or pressure, especially with effort.\nSudden weakness or numbness on one side, drooping face or trouble speaking.\nUnexplained severe muscle pain, weakness or dark urine while on cholesterol medicine -- have this checked promptly.\nSevere belly pain, which can happen when triglycerides are very high.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW()),

('Underactive thyroid', 'E03', 'hypothyroidism,underactive thyroid,low thyroid',
 'The thyroid gland in the neck is making less thyroid hormone than the body needs. That hormone sets the pace of the body, so when it is low, everything runs slower.',
 'Most often the immune system has gradually damaged the gland. It can also follow thyroid surgery, radioactive iodine treatment, some medicines, or iodine lack.',
 'Tiredness, feeling cold, weight gain, dry skin, constipation, hair thinning, low mood, poor memory, and heavier or irregular periods.',
 'Take the tablet once a day on an empty stomach, with water, and wait 30 to 60 minutes before eating.\nTake it at the same time every day; first thing in the morning is easiest to remember.\nHave the blood test repeated when asked, as the dose is set by the test and not by how you feel.\nTell your doctor if you become pregnant or are planning to -- the dose usually needs to change quickly.\nKeep to the same brand if you can, and mention any switch.',
 'Do not take it at the same time as iron, calcium, antacids, or multivitamins; leave at least four hours.\nDo not stop or change the dose yourself when symptoms improve.\nDo not double up a dose to catch up for a missed one unless you were told you may.\nDo not rely on iodine supplements or seaweed products to treat it.',
 'Chest pain or a fast or pounding heartbeat after a dose change.\nFeeling increasingly cold, very drowsy or confused.\nA neck lump that is growing, or new hoarseness, or trouble swallowing or breathing.\nPregnancy -- tell the clinic straight away.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW()),

('Overactive thyroid', 'E05', 'hyperthyroidism,overactive thyroid,thyrotoxicosis,graves disease,graves',
 'The thyroid gland is making more hormone than the body needs, so the body runs too fast: the heart beats quickly, weight falls, and everything feels speeded up.',
 'Most commonly the immune system is driving the gland to overwork. A lumpy overactive gland, thyroid inflammation, or too much thyroid medicine can also do it.',
 'Weight loss while eating normally, a fast or pounding heartbeat, feeling hot and sweaty, shaky hands, anxiety and irritability, poor sleep, loose stools, and sometimes staring or bulging eyes.',
 'Take your medicine exactly as prescribed and keep every blood test appointment.\nRest while your heart rate is high, and build activity back gradually.\nEat well; a good appetite with weight loss still means the body is burning through its reserves.\nIf your eyes are affected, use the lubricating drops you were given, wear sunglasses outdoors, and raise the head of the bed.\nTell any other doctor treating you that your thyroid is overactive, especially before surgery.',
 'Do not stop the medicine when you feel better; stopping early usually lets it come straight back.\nAvoid caffeine and energy drinks while your heart is racing.\nDo not take iodine supplements, seaweed or kelp products.\nDo not smoke, which makes thyroid eye problems considerably worse.',
 'Fever with a very fast heartbeat, agitation or confusion, or vomiting -- this is an emergency.\nChest pain, severe breathlessness, or fainting.\nA sore throat with fever, or mouth ulcers, while on thyroid medicine: this needs a same-day blood count.\nYellowing of the eyes or skin, or dark urine.\nSudden loss or doubling of vision, or an eye that is painful and pushed forward.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW()),

-- ---------------- chest and breathing ----------------
('Pneumonia', 'J18', 'pneumonia,community-acquired pneumonia,cap,lung infection,hap',
 'An infection in the lung tissue itself. The small air sacs fill with fluid and pus, so that part of the lung cannot take up oxygen properly.',
 'Usually bacteria or a virus breathed in, or an infection that followed on from flu or a cold. Smoking, long-term lung disease, diabetes, old age and a weak immune system all make it easier to catch.',
 'Cough with phlegm, fever and chills, chest pain that is worse when breathing in, breathlessness, and feeling very tired and unwell.',
 'Finish the whole course of antibiotics, even after you feel better.\nRest, and drink plenty of fluids to loosen the phlegm.\nTake paracetamol for fever and aches as directed.\nSit up and take slow deep breaths several times an hour, and cough the phlegm out rather than holding it in.\nGo to the follow-up visit and have the repeat chest x-ray if one was ordered.\nAsk about flu and pneumonia vaccination once you have recovered.\nExpect the tiredness to take several weeks to lift, and come back to normal activity gradually.',
 'Do not stop the antibiotic early because the fever has gone -- the infection can come back harder.\nDo not smoke, and stay away from smoke and fumes while the lung heals.\nDo not take cough suppressants to stop a productive cough unless your doctor advised it.\nDo not go back to heavy work or exercise while still breathless.',
 'Breathlessness at rest, or breathing fast, or lips or fingertips turning bluish.\nFever that is still there after three days of antibiotics, or that returns.\nConfusion, drowsiness, or fainting.\nCoughing up blood, or chest pain that is getting worse.\nNot able to keep fluids down, or passing very little urine.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW()),

('Acute bronchitis', 'J20', 'bronchitis,acute bronchitis,chest infection,tracheobronchitis',
 'The airways that carry air into the lungs are inflamed and swollen after an infection, so they make extra mucus and you cough. The lung tissue itself is not infected, which is what separates this from pneumonia.',
 'Nearly always a virus, often following a cold. Smoke, dust and fumes make it worse and make it last longer.',
 'A cough that may be dry at first then bring up phlegm, chest tightness, wheeze, mild fever and a sore throat. The cough often lingers two to three weeks after everything else has settled.',
 'Rest and drink plenty of warm fluids.\nTake paracetamol for fever and aches as directed.\nUse steam from a basin of hot water or a warm shower to loosen phlegm.\nSleep propped up if the cough is worse lying flat.\nExpect the cough to outlast the illness by a couple of weeks, and let it clear gradually.',
 'Do not expect or ask for antibiotics for a viral bronchitis; they do not help and bring side effects.\nDo not smoke or vape, and avoid smoky, dusty or fumy air.\nDo not suppress a phlegmy cough with cough syrup unless advised -- the cough is clearing the airway.\nDo not share your medicine with family members who are also coughing.',
 'Breathlessness at rest, or breathing fast, or a wheeze you cannot settle.\nFever above 38 degrees that lasts more than three days, or returns after settling.\nCoughing up blood, or chest pain that worsens with each breath.\nA cough still there after three weeks, or getting worse rather than better.\nIn someone with asthma, COPD, heart disease or a weak immune system, get checked early rather than waiting.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW()),

('Asthma', 'J45', 'asthma,bronchial asthma,reactive airway disease,asthma exacerbation',
 'The airways are sensitive and easily inflamed. When something sets them off they tighten, swell and fill with mucus, which narrows them and makes breathing out hard. Between attacks they can be completely normal.',
 'The tendency often runs in families and goes with allergies and eczema. Attacks are set off by dust mites, cockroach and animal dander, pollen, smoke, strong smells, cold air, exercise, infections, stress and some medicines.',
 'Wheezing, cough that is often worse at night or early morning, chest tightness, and breathlessness that comes and goes.',
 'Use your controller inhaler every day as prescribed, even when you feel completely well -- it is what prevents attacks.\nCarry your reliever inhaler with you at all times.\nLearn the correct inhaler technique and have someone check it; most people lose half the dose to poor technique.\nUse a spacer if you were given one, and rinse your mouth after a steroid inhaler.\nKeep a written action plan at home, and know your own early warning signs.\nReduce dust at home: wash bedding weekly in hot water, damp-wipe rather than dry-sweep, deal with cockroaches and damp.\nHave a flu vaccination each year.',
 'Do not rely on the reliever inhaler alone; needing it more than twice a week means the asthma is not controlled.\nDo not stop the controller inhaler because you feel fine.\nDo not smoke or vape, and keep smoke out of the house and the car.\nDo not use anyone else''s inhaler or an old expired one.\nDo not delay getting help once an attack is not responding to the reliever.',
 'Breathlessness so bad you cannot speak a full sentence, walk, or lie flat.\nThe reliever inhaler not working, or wearing off in under two to three hours.\nLips or fingertips turning bluish, or chest and neck pulling in with each breath.\nA child too breathless to feed, talk or play, or becoming drowsy.\nA quiet chest with no wheeze but severe breathlessness -- this is more serious, not less.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW()),

('Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)', 'J44', 'copd,chronic obstructive pulmonary disease,emphysema,chronic bronchitis,copd exacerbation',
 'The airways are permanently narrowed and the air sacs damaged, so air gets trapped and emptying the lungs takes effort. Unlike asthma this does not fully reverse, but it can be slowed and managed well.',
 'Long-term smoking is the main cause. Years of woodsmoke or cooking smoke, workplace dust and fumes, and repeated childhood chest infections also contribute.',
 'Breathlessness that slowly worsens over years, a long-standing cough with phlegm, wheeze, chest tightness, and chest infections that keep coming back.',
 'Stop smoking -- this is the single thing that changes the course of the disease, at any age and any stage.\nTake your inhalers every day as prescribed, with the right technique and a spacer if given.\nKeep walking and stay as active as you can; ask about a pulmonary rehabilitation programme.\nHave flu vaccination every year and pneumonia vaccination as advised.\nLearn your own warning signs of a flare-up, and start your action plan early.\nIf you are on home oxygen, use it for the number of hours prescribed.\nEat well and in smaller, more frequent meals if a full stomach makes breathing harder.',
 'Do not smoke, and do not let anyone smoke indoors near you.\nAvoid woodsmoke, cooking smoke, dust and strong fumes; ventilate the kitchen.\nDo not turn your oxygen up on your own -- more is not better and can be harmful.\nDo not sit still for days when breathless; inactivity makes the breathlessness worse.\nDo not wait out a flare-up that is not improving.',
 'Breathlessness much worse than your usual, or at rest.\nPhlegm turning yellow, green or bloody, or much greater in amount.\nFever, or new chest pain.\nAnkles swelling, or morning headaches, or drowsiness and confusion.\nLips or fingertips turning bluish.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW()),

('Pulmonary tuberculosis', 'A15', 'tuberculosis,tb,ptb,pulmonary tuberculosis,koch infection',
 'An infection of the lungs by the TB bacterium. It grows slowly, which is why the illness builds over weeks rather than days. It is curable, but only with a full course of several medicines taken for months.',
 'It spreads through the air when someone with untreated TB of the lungs coughs or sneezes. Close, crowded, poorly ventilated living and working conditions, diabetes, HIV, smoking and poor nutrition all raise the risk.',
 'A cough lasting more than two weeks, coughing up phlegm or blood, fever especially in the afternoon, drenching night sweats, weight loss and loss of appetite, and chest pain.',
 'Take every single dose for the whole number of months prescribed -- this is the entire difference between cure and drug-resistant TB.\nKeep to your treatment supporter or DOTS schedule, and go for every sputum check and follow-up.\nCover your mouth and nose when coughing, use a tissue or your sleeve, and dispose of tissues in a closed bag.\nOpen windows and doors; TB does not survive well in fresh air and sunlight.\nSleep in a separate, well-ventilated room during the first weeks of treatment if you can.\nBring your household and close contacts, especially children, for screening.\nEat well -- weight gain is a sign treatment is working.',
 'Do not stop treatment when you start to feel better, usually after a few weeks; stopping is what creates drug-resistant TB.\nDo not miss doses or take them irregularly, and do not restart an old leftover course on your own.\nDo not share your medicine with anyone.\nDo not smoke or drink alcohol during treatment; both harm the liver that is processing the medicines.\nDo not hide the diagnosis from people you live with -- they need screening.',
 'Coughing up a large amount of blood.\nYellowing of the eyes or skin, dark urine, persistent vomiting, or severe belly pain -- these can mean the medicines are affecting your liver.\nA new rash, fever, or severe itching after starting treatment.\nChanges in your vision or colour vision.\nBreathlessness, severe chest pain, or a cough and fever that worsen after treatment had been going well.\nNumbness or burning in the hands or feet.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW()),

('Influenza (flu)', 'J11', 'influenza,flu,trangkaso,seasonal flu',
 'A viral infection of the nose, throat and airways. It comes on more suddenly and hits harder than a common cold, with fever and body aches rather than just a blocked nose.',
 'A flu virus passed on in coughs, sneezes and on hands and surfaces. It spreads easily in crowded indoor places.',
 'Sudden fever and chills, headache, muscle and joint aches, dry cough, sore throat, blocked or runny nose, and marked tiredness that can linger a week or two.',
 'Rest at home and keep away from others until the fever has gone for a full day.\nDrink plenty of fluids.\nTake paracetamol for fever and aches as directed.\nWash your hands often, and cover coughs and sneezes.\nAsk about yearly flu vaccination, especially for children, older people, pregnant women and anyone with a long-term illness.',
 'Do not expect antibiotics; they do not work against a virus.\nDo not give aspirin to a child or teenager with a fever.\nDo not go to work or school while feverish.\nDo not smoke, which makes the cough and recovery worse.',
 'Breathlessness, chest pain, or breathing fast.\nFever lasting more than three days, or a fever that settles and then returns with a worse cough.\nConfusion, severe drowsiness, or a seizure.\nNot able to keep fluids down, or passing very little urine.\nIn a baby: poor feeding, no wet nappies, floppiness, or a high-pitched continuous cry.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW()),

('COVID-19', 'U07.1', 'covid,covid-19,sars-cov-2,coronavirus disease',
 'An infection caused by the SARS-CoV-2 virus. Most people have a mild illness of the nose, throat and airways, but it can affect the lungs and other organs, particularly in older people and those with other conditions.',
 'The virus spreads in the air and in droplets from coughing, sneezing, talking and singing, especially in crowded or poorly ventilated indoor spaces.',
 'Fever, cough, sore throat, blocked or runny nose, headache, body aches, tiredness, and loss of taste or smell. Some have diarrhoea or no symptoms at all.',
 'Rest, drink plenty of fluids, and take paracetamol for fever and aches as directed.\nStay in a separate well-ventilated room and wear a mask around others while you are infectious.\nOpen windows to air out shared rooms.\nWatch your breathing, and check your oxygen level if you were given a pulse oximeter.\nKeep up with the vaccination and booster schedule advised for you.\nCome back gradually: tiredness can last for weeks.',
 'Do not take antibiotics, ivermectin or other unproven remedies for it.\nDo not visit older or unwell relatives while you are still infectious.\nDo not go back to hard exercise while still breathless or very tired.\nDo not ignore breathlessness because the rest of the illness feels mild.',
 'Breathlessness at rest or on light effort, or breathing fast.\nAn oxygen reading below 95 percent, or falling when you walk about.\nChest pain or pressure.\nConfusion, drowsiness, or difficulty waking.\nBluish lips or face.\nNot able to keep fluids down.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW()),

-- ---------------- stomach, bowel and liver ----------------
('Acute gastroenteritis', 'A09', 'gastroenteritis,age,acute gastroenteritis,diarrhea,diarrhoea,loose bowel movement,lbm,stomach flu',
 'An infection of the stomach and bowel that causes diarrhoea and vomiting. Most of it settles by itself in a few days; the thing that actually matters is replacing the fluid you are losing.',
 'A virus, bacteria or parasite, usually from contaminated food or water, or passed on by unwashed hands.',
 'Watery stools, stomach cramps, nausea and vomiting, mild fever, headache and weakness.',
 'Drink oral rehydration solution in small frequent sips -- this is the treatment, and plain water alone does not replace the salts lost.\nKeep eating as soon as you can manage it: rice, banana, bread, soup, crackers.\nKeep breastfeeding or formula feeding a baby, and offer extra fluid between feeds.\nWash hands with soap after the toilet and before preparing food.\nRest, and stay off work or school until 48 hours after the last episode.',
 'Do not take medicine to stop the diarrhoea unless your doctor told you to, especially if there is fever or blood in the stool.\nDo not drink softdrinks, undiluted juice or sports drinks to rehydrate -- the sugar pulls more water into the bowel.\nDo not take antibiotics left over from before.\nDo not prepare food for other people while you are ill.\nDo not give a child plain water alone in large amounts instead of rehydration solution.',
 'Signs of drying out: very little or no urine, dry mouth, sunken eyes, no tears, dizziness on standing, drowsiness.\nBlood or mucus in the stool, or black stool.\nVomiting everything, so that nothing stays down for more than a few hours.\nSevere or constant belly pain, or a swollen tender belly.\nFever above 38.5 degrees, or diarrhoea lasting more than three days.\nIn a baby: a sunken soft spot, no wet nappies for six hours, floppiness or constant crying.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW()),

('Acid reflux (GERD)', 'K21', 'gerd,gastroesophageal reflux,acid reflux,reflux,heartburn,hyperacidity',
 'Acid from the stomach travels back up into the food pipe, which is not built to cope with it. That is what causes the burning behind the breastbone and the sour taste.',
 'The muscle ring at the bottom of the food pipe is not closing tightly enough. Large meals, late eating, extra weight, smoking, alcohol, coffee, spicy and fatty food, and pregnancy all make it more likely.',
 'Burning behind the breastbone, especially after meals or lying down, a sour or bitter taste, belching, a feeling of food coming back up, a persistent dry cough or hoarseness, and sometimes chest discomfort.',
 'Eat smaller meals, more often, and eat slowly.\nFinish your last meal three hours before lying down.\nRaise the head of the bed by about 15 to 20 centimetres, or sleep on a wedge -- extra pillows alone bend you in the middle and do not help.\nLose some weight if you are carrying extra, and avoid tight belts and waistbands.\nTake acid-reducing medicine as prescribed, usually 30 to 60 minutes before a meal.\nKeep a note of which foods set you off; they differ from person to person.',
 'Do not lie down or sleep straight after eating.\nCut down on coffee, chocolate, very spicy and fatty food, tomato, citrus, mint, softdrinks and alcohol if they affect you.\nDo not smoke.\nDo not take anti-inflammatory painkillers on an empty stomach, or regularly, without asking.\nDo not keep treating it with antacids for months without being reviewed.',
 'Trouble or pain on swallowing, or food sticking.\nVomiting blood, or stool that is black and tarry.\nLosing weight without trying, or vomiting that keeps happening.\nChest pain with sweating, breathlessness, or pain spreading to the arm or jaw -- treat this as a possible heart problem, not reflux.\nSymptoms that are not settling despite treatment.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW()),

('Gastritis', 'K29', 'gastritis,acute gastritis,stomach inflammation,hyperacidity,dyspepsia',
 'The lining of the stomach is inflamed and sore. It is the lining''s protective layer being overwhelmed, which is why it hurts most when the stomach is empty or irritated.',
 'Commonly a bacterium called H. pylori, regular anti-inflammatory painkillers, alcohol, smoking, severe stress or serious illness, and sometimes very spicy or irregular eating.',
 'Gnawing or burning pain in the upper belly, fullness after a little food, nausea, belching, bloating and loss of appetite.',
 'Eat small regular meals rather than one or two large ones, and do not let the stomach stay empty for long.\nTake the medicines as prescribed, and if you were given treatment for H. pylori, finish the whole course exactly.\nUse paracetamol rather than anti-inflammatory painkillers for pain, unless told otherwise.\nCut down alcohol and stop smoking.\nFind a way to manage stress and sleep; both show up in the stomach.',
 'Do not take anti-inflammatory painkillers such as those for body pain or arthritis unless your doctor has said you may.\nDo not drink alcohol or smoke while the lining is healing.\nDo not skip meals or eat very late.\nDo not stop the H. pylori course early -- it will not clear and will come back.\nDo not self-treat for months with over-the-counter antacids.',
 'Vomiting blood, or material that looks like coffee grounds.\nBlack, tarry stool.\nSevere or sudden upper belly pain, or pain going through to the back.\nLosing weight without trying, or being unable to keep food down.\nFeeling faint, very weak, or short of breath, which can mean blood loss.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW()),

('Peptic ulcer', 'K27', 'peptic ulcer,gastric ulcer,duodenal ulcer,ulcer,pud',
 'An open sore in the lining of the stomach or the first part of the small bowel, where acid has worn through the protective layer.',
 'Most are caused by the H. pylori bacterium or by regular use of anti-inflammatory painkillers. Smoking and alcohol slow healing; stress and spicy food make the pain worse rather than causing the ulcer.',
 'Burning or gnawing upper belly pain, often when hungry or at night, relieved for a while by food or antacids; also bloating, nausea, and feeling full quickly.',
 'Take the full course of medicine exactly as prescribed, including every antibiotic if you were treated for H. pylori.\nEat regular meals and avoid long gaps without food.\nUse paracetamol for pain instead of anti-inflammatory painkillers, unless your doctor said otherwise.\nStop smoking, which roughly doubles the time an ulcer takes to heal.\nGo for the follow-up or repeat test to confirm it has healed and the bacterium is cleared.',
 'Do not take anti-inflammatory painkillers or aspirin unless your doctor has specifically approved it.\nDo not drink alcohol while it is healing.\nDo not stop the antibiotic course early.\nDo not ignore a change in the pattern of the pain.',
 'Vomiting blood, or material like coffee grounds.\nBlack tarry stool, or fresh blood in the stool.\nSudden severe belly pain, with a hard board-like belly -- this is an emergency.\nFeeling faint, sweaty, breathless or very weak.\nPersistent vomiting, or losing weight without trying.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW()),

('Gallstones', 'K80', 'gallstones,cholelithiasis,cholecystitis,gallbladder stones,biliary colic',
 'Hard stones have formed in the gallbladder, the small bag under the liver that stores bile. Pain comes when a stone blocks the outlet, usually after a fatty meal, and the gallbladder can become inflamed and infected.',
 'Bile that is too concentrated forms crystals and then stones. It is commoner with age, in women, with pregnancy, with extra weight, with rapid weight loss, and where it runs in the family.',
 'Steady severe pain in the upper right belly or just below the breastbone, often spreading to the right shoulder blade, with nausea and vomiting; typically after eating something fatty and lasting from 30 minutes to a few hours.',
 'Eat low-fat meals while you are waiting for treatment: grill, boil or steam, and keep portions modest.\nTake the pain relief you were prescribed, and keep a note of how often attacks come.\nDrink enough water.\nIf surgery has been advised, keep the appointment -- attacks tend to repeat and get worse.\nAfter gallbladder surgery, start with light low-fat food and build up over a couple of weeks, and walk a little each day.',
 'Avoid fatty, fried and creamy food: lechon, chicharon, fast food, coconut-milk dishes, pastries.\nDo not go on a crash diet; fast weight loss makes more stones.\nDo not treat repeated attacks at home with painkillers alone and hope they stop.\nDo not ignore fever with the pain.',
 'Pain lasting more than a few hours, or much more severe than before.\nFever and chills with the belly pain.\nYellowing of the eyes or skin, dark urine, or pale stools.\nPersistent vomiting, or a swollen tender belly.\nPain going through to the back with vomiting, which can mean the pancreas is involved.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW()),

('Appendicitis', 'K35', 'appendicitis,appendix,acute appendicitis,appendectomy',
 'The appendix, a small tube attached to the start of the large bowel, has become blocked and infected. It swells, and if it is not removed it can burst and spread infection through the belly.',
 'Something blocks the opening of the appendix -- hardened stool, swollen lymph tissue after an infection -- and bacteria multiply behind the blockage.',
 'Pain that often starts around the navel and settles in the lower right belly over some hours, worse on moving, coughing or pressing; with loss of appetite, nausea, vomiting and a mild fever.',
 'If surgery is planned, do not eat or drink from the time you are told, and tell the team about all your medicines and allergies.\nAfter the operation, get up and walk a little as soon as you are allowed -- it prevents chest and clot problems.\nTake the pain relief and any antibiotics as prescribed.\nKeep the wound clean and dry, and follow the advice you were given about showering and dressings.\nStart with light food and build up; avoid straining, and treat constipation early.\nAvoid heavy lifting and strenuous work for the period your surgeon specified.',
 'Do not take painkillers and wait at home once this has been suspected; a delay is what leads to a burst appendix.\nDo not eat or drink if you have been told you are for surgery.\nDo not apply a hot compress to the belly or take a laxative or enema for the pain.\nDo not lift heavy things or return to hard physical work before you were cleared.',
 'Pain that suddenly becomes much worse, or spreads across the whole belly.\nFever with chills, or a belly that is hard and tender all over.\nPersistent vomiting, or not passing wind or stool at all.\nAfter surgery: fever, a wound that is red, swollen, opening or leaking, or pain that increases rather than settles.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW()),

('Chronic hepatitis B', 'B18', 'hepatitis b,hbv,chronic hepatitis,hepa b',
 'A long-term infection of the liver with the hepatitis B virus. Many people feel completely well for years, while the virus can still slowly damage the liver, which is why monitoring matters even without symptoms.',
 'The virus passes through blood and body fluids: from mother to baby at birth, through unprotected sex, and through shared needles or unsterile instruments. It is not spread by sharing food or by hugging.',
 'Often none for years. Later: tiredness, loss of appetite, nausea, belly discomfort, yellowing of the eyes or skin, dark urine, and swelling of the legs or belly.',
 'Go to every monitoring appointment, including blood tests and liver scans, even while you feel perfectly well.\nTake antiviral medicine every day exactly as prescribed, if you were started on it.\nHave the people you live with and your sexual partners tested and vaccinated.\nTell any doctor, dentist or surgeon treating you that you have hepatitis B.\nAvoid alcohol completely, and check with your doctor before any new medicine, supplement or herbal product.\nKeep to a healthy weight; fatty liver on top of hepatitis B does more harm.',
 'Do not stop antiviral medicine on your own -- the virus can rebound and harm the liver.\nDo not drink alcohol.\nDo not take herbal or unlabelled supplements for the liver; several are harmful to it.\nDo not share razors, toothbrushes, needles or nail cutters.\nDo not skip monitoring because you feel well; liver cancer screening depends on it.',
 'Yellowing of the eyes or skin, or dark urine.\nVomiting blood, or black tarry stool.\nA swollen belly, or swelling of both legs.\nConfusion, unusual drowsiness or disturbed sleep pattern.\nSevere tiredness with loss of appetite and weight.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW()),

-- ---------------- kidneys and urinary ----------------
('Urinary tract infection', 'N39.0', 'uti,urinary tract infection,bladder infection,cystitis,dysuria',
 'An infection in the urinary system, most often the bladder. Bacteria that normally live in the bowel get into the urethra and multiply in urine.',
 'Commoner in women because the urethra is short. Holding urine for long periods, not drinking enough, sexual activity, pregnancy, diabetes, a catheter, and anything that blocks urine flow all make it easier.',
 'Burning or stinging when passing urine, needing to go often and urgently, passing only small amounts, cloudy or strong-smelling urine, lower belly discomfort, and sometimes blood in the urine.',
 'Finish the whole course of antibiotics, even once the burning has stopped.\nDrink plenty of water through the day to flush the bladder.\nPass urine when you feel the need rather than holding it, and empty the bladder fully.\nWomen: wipe from front to back, and pass urine soon after sex.\nTake paracetamol for the discomfort as directed.\nCome back if it keeps returning -- that is worth looking into rather than treating again and again.',
 'Do not stop the antibiotic early; a half-treated infection comes back and becomes harder to treat.\nDo not use leftover antibiotics or someone else''s.\nDo not hold urine for long periods.\nAvoid scented soaps, douches and bubble baths in the genital area.\nDo not ignore it in pregnancy, in a man, or in a child -- all three need proper assessment.',
 'Fever with chills, or pain in the back or side just below the ribs -- the infection may have reached the kidney.\nVomiting, or being unable to keep fluids or medicine down.\nBlood in the urine, or passing no urine at all.\nSymptoms not improving after two to three days of antibiotics.\nAny UTI in pregnancy, or in a baby or young child.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW()),

('Kidney infection (pyelonephritis)', 'N10', 'pyelonephritis,kidney infection,acute pyelonephritis,renal infection',
 'An infection that has travelled up from the bladder into the kidney itself. It is more serious than a bladder infection because the kidney is working tissue and the infection can get into the blood.',
 'Usually bacteria moving up from a bladder infection. A stone, an enlarged prostate, pregnancy, diabetes, a catheter, or anything slowing urine flow makes it more likely.',
 'Fever and chills, pain in the back or side below the ribs, nausea and vomiting, feeling very unwell, along with burning and frequent urination.',
 'Take the full course of antibiotics; this course is usually longer than for a bladder infection, and finishing it matters.\nDrink plenty of fluids unless you were told to limit them.\nRest, and take paracetamol for fever and pain as directed.\nGo to the follow-up, and have the scan or urine test if one was ordered -- a stone or blockage behind the infection needs finding.\nExpect to feel tired for a week or two afterwards.',
 'Do not stop the antibiotic once the fever settles.\nDo not manage fever with back pain at home for several days before coming in.\nDo not take anti-inflammatory painkillers for the pain unless approved; they can be hard on the kidney.\nDo not ignore it coming back -- a second episode needs investigation.',
 'Fever still above 38 degrees after 48 hours of antibiotics.\nVomiting so that you cannot keep fluids or tablets down.\nSevere back or side pain, or pain with a swollen tender belly.\nPassing very little or no urine.\nConfusion, drowsiness, a very fast heartbeat, or feeling faint -- this can mean the infection is in the blood.\nIn pregnancy, any of the above, urgently.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW()),

('Chronic kidney disease', 'N18', 'chronic kidney disease,ckd,renal failure,kidney failure,renal insufficiency',
 'The kidneys are filtering less well than they should, and have been for some time. They clear waste and extra water from the blood and help control blood pressure, red blood cells and bone health, so all of those are affected.',
 'Most often years of diabetes or high blood pressure. Also repeated kidney infections, blockages and stones, some inherited conditions, and long-term use of certain painkillers.',
 'Often nothing in the early stages. Later: tiredness, poor appetite, nausea, swollen ankles and face, itching, passing urine more at night, poor sleep and breathlessness.',
 'Take your blood pressure and other medicines every day as prescribed -- controlling blood pressure is the main way of slowing this down.\nKeep blood sugar well controlled if you have diabetes.\nFollow the diet you were given, especially the limits on salt, and on potassium, phosphate and protein if those were specified.\nStick to any fluid limit you were set.\nGo to every clinic visit and blood test; the numbers change before you feel anything.\nAsk before taking any new medicine, including ones from the pharmacy.',
 'Do not take anti-inflammatory painkillers for body pain; they are among the commonest causes of further kidney damage.\nDo not take herbal preparations, slimming products or unlabelled supplements.\nAvoid salty food and salt substitutes, which are often high in potassium.\nDo not use someone else''s medicines, and do not stop your own because you feel well.\nDo not smoke.',
 'Passing much less urine, or none.\nBreathlessness, or swelling that is getting worse quickly.\nVomiting that will not stop, or hiccups that go on.\nMuscle weakness, numbness, or an irregular heartbeat -- these can mean potassium is dangerously high.\nConfusion or drowsiness.\nChest pain.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW()),

-- ---------------- infections common in the Philippines ----------------
('Dengue', 'A90', 'dengue,dengue fever,dengue hemorrhagic fever,dhf,breakbone fever',
 'A viral infection carried by mosquitoes. Most people recover, but there is a critical window just as the fever drops -- usually day three to seven -- when fluid can leak from blood vessels and the illness can become dangerous. That is why follow-up during that window matters.',
 'The dengue virus, spread by the bite of an infected Aedes mosquito. These mosquitoes breed in clean standing water around the home and bite mostly in the early morning and late afternoon.',
 'Sudden high fever, severe headache, pain behind the eyes, muscle and joint pain, nausea and vomiting, and a rash. Later: easy bruising, bleeding gums or nosebleeds.',
 'Drink plenty of fluids: water, oral rehydration solution, soups, buko juice. Keeping hydrated is the main treatment at home.\nTake paracetamol only, for fever and pain, exactly as directed.\nRest, and have someone stay with you who can watch for warning signs.\nGo for repeat blood counts as often as your doctor asks, especially around the time the fever settles.\nWatch most carefully in the day or two after the fever drops -- feeling better then does not mean it is over.\nClear standing water around the house: pots, tyres, gutters, drums, plant trays. Use screens, repellent and long sleeves.',
 'Do not take aspirin, mefenamic acid, ibuprofen or any anti-inflammatory painkiller -- they increase the risk of bleeding.\nDo not assume you are safe once the fever goes; that is when the critical phase begins.\nDo not stay home and wait if any warning sign appears.\nDo not treat it with antibiotics, which do nothing to a virus.',
 'Severe belly pain, or persistent vomiting.\nBleeding: gums, nose, bruising, black stool, blood in vomit or urine, or unusually heavy menstrual bleeding.\nRestlessness, irritability, drowsiness, or cold clammy hands and feet.\nBreathlessness, or a swollen belly.\nNot passing urine for six to eight hours.\nAny of these means the emergency department immediately.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW()),

('Typhoid fever', 'A01', 'typhoid,typhoid fever,enteric fever,salmonella typhi',
 'An infection of the bloodstream and bowel caused by bacteria swallowed in contaminated food or water. The fever climbs step by step over days rather than spiking at once.',
 'Salmonella Typhi bacteria from food or water contaminated by human waste, often through unwashed hands during food preparation. Someone can carry and pass on the bacteria without being ill.',
 'Fever that rises over several days, headache, weakness and tiredness, belly pain, constipation or diarrhoea, poor appetite, and sometimes a faint rash.',
 'Finish the entire course of antibiotics; stopping early allows relapse and carriage.\nDrink plenty of fluids and eat soft, easily digested food in small amounts.\nRest; the weakness takes a while to pass.\nWash hands with soap thoroughly after the toilet and before handling any food.\nStay away from preparing food for other people until your doctor says it is safe.\nHave the household checked if anyone else develops fever.',
 'Do not stop antibiotics when the fever settles -- relapse is common and the bacteria can persist.\nDo not prepare or serve food for others while you are ill or until cleared.\nDo not take anti-diarrhoeal medicine unless your doctor advised it.\nAvoid street food, untreated water and ice from unknown sources during recovery.',
 'Severe belly pain, a hard or very tender belly -- the bowel can perforate, which is an emergency.\nBlood in the stool, or black tarry stool.\nConfusion, extreme drowsiness, or delirium.\nPersistent vomiting, or being unable to keep fluids down.\nFever returning after it had settled.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW()),

('Leptospirosis', 'A27', 'leptospirosis,lepto,weil disease,rat urine disease',
 'A bacterial infection caught from water or soil contaminated with the urine of infected animals, usually rats. It can be mild, but it can also affect the kidneys, liver and lungs, so it is treated seriously.',
 'Leptospira bacteria enter through broken skin, or through the eyes, nose or mouth, typically after wading in floodwater or working in wet soil, canals or rice fields.',
 'Fever and chills, severe headache, marked muscle pain especially in the calves, red eyes, nausea and vomiting; later, yellowing of the eyes, reduced urine, or coughing blood.',
 'Take the full course of antibiotics exactly as prescribed, and start them as early as possible.\nRest and drink plenty of fluids unless you were told to limit them.\nGo for the blood tests that check your kidneys and liver, and keep the follow-up.\nTell your doctor if you waded in floodwater, as that guides treatment.\nIn future floods: wear boots, cover any wound, and wash with soap and clean water as soon as you can.\nAsk about preventive medicine if you must wade in floodwater again.',
 'Do not wade in floodwater with open wounds, cuts or scratches.\nDo not take anti-inflammatory painkillers for the muscle pain; they can be hard on the kidneys here.\nDo not stop the antibiotic early.\nDo not wait at home with fever after flood exposure -- early treatment is what prevents the severe form.',
 'Passing much less urine, or none.\nYellowing of the eyes or skin.\nCoughing up blood, or breathlessness.\nBleeding from the gums or nose, or bruising.\nConfusion, drowsiness, or a stiff neck with severe headache.\nChest pain or an irregular heartbeat.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW()),

('Animal bite and rabies risk', 'Z20.3', 'animal bite,dog bite,cat bite,rabies exposure,rabies,bite wound',
 'A bite or scratch from a dog, cat or other mammal can introduce bacteria, and in some animals the rabies virus. Rabies is almost always fatal once symptoms start, and almost completely preventable if the vaccine course is given and finished in time.',
 'The virus is in the saliva of an infected animal and enters through a bite, a scratch, or a lick on broken skin or the eyes, nose or mouth.',
 'The wound itself may be small. Rabies, if it develops, begins weeks to months later with tingling at the bite, fever, anxiety, difficulty swallowing and fear of water -- by then it cannot be treated.',
 'Wash the wound immediately with soap and plenty of running water for at least 15 minutes -- this alone removes much of the virus.\nGo to an animal bite centre the same day, even for a small scratch.\nComplete every dose of the vaccine course on the exact dates given; write them on a calendar.\nHave the immunoglobulin as well if it was advised for your category of bite.\nKeep the wound clean, and take any antibiotics and the tetanus injection as prescribed.\nHave the animal observed for 14 days if it can be found, and report the bite.',
 'Do not skip or delay any dose of the vaccine course; a missed dose can make it ineffective.\nDo not wait to see whether the animal gets sick before starting treatment.\nDo not apply garlic, oil, herbs, toothpaste or a tourniquet to the wound.\nDo not let the wound be stitched tightly closed unless your doctor decided so.\nDo not assume a pet, a puppy, or a vaccinated animal carries no risk.',
 'The wound becoming red, swollen, hot, or leaking pus, or a red streak spreading from it.\nFever after a bite.\nA bite on the face, head, neck, hand or genitals, or a deep or multiple bite -- these need same-day attention.\nAny tingling or numbness at the site of a healed bite, difficulty swallowing, or fear of water -- emergency, immediately.\nNo tetanus cover in the last five years.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW()),

('Sepsis', 'A41', 'sepsis,septicemia,severe sepsis,septic shock,bacteremia,blood infection',
 'Sepsis is the body''s reaction to an infection going too far: instead of fighting only the infection, the response begins to damage the body''s own organs. It is a medical emergency and every hour of treatment counts.',
 'Any infection can lead to it -- lung, urine, skin, bowel, or a wound. It is more likely in the very young and very old, in diabetes, after surgery, and where the immune system is weak.',
 'Fever or an unusually low temperature, shivering, a fast heartbeat, fast breathing, confusion or slurred speech, extreme pain or discomfort, clammy or mottled skin, and passing little urine.',
 'Finish all antibiotics exactly as prescribed after you go home.\nRest and drink enough fluid; recovery takes weeks to months and tiredness is normal.\nGo to every follow-up, and ask about rehabilitation if you are weak.\nTreat infections early from now on, and keep vaccinations up to date.\nKeep any wound or catheter site clean, and have it reviewed as arranged.\nTell the household what the warning signs of sepsis are, so they act quickly another time.',
 'Do not stop the antibiotic course early.\nDo not ignore a new fever during recovery.\nDo not expect to return to full strength quickly, and do not push into heavy work before you are able.\nDo not delay getting help for a new infection.',
 'Fever with confusion, slurred speech or drowsiness.\nBreathing fast, or breathlessness at rest.\nSkin that is mottled, bluish or very pale, or a rash that does not fade when pressed.\nPassing no urine for many hours.\nFeeling that something is very seriously wrong -- say the word sepsis at the desk and ask to be seen immediately.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW()),

-- ---------------- blood ----------------
('Iron deficiency anaemia', 'D50', 'anemia,anaemia,iron deficiency anemia,low hemoglobin,low blood',
 'There is not enough haemoglobin in your blood to carry oxygen properly, because the body is short of iron to build it. That is why the main symptom is tiredness.',
 'Blood loss is the commonest reason: heavy periods, bleeding from the stomach or bowel, or hookworm. Also too little iron in the diet, pregnancy, and poor absorption of iron.',
 'Tiredness and weakness, pale skin, breathlessness on effort, dizziness, headache, cold hands and feet, brittle nails, hair fall, and sometimes a craving to chew ice.',
 'Take the iron exactly as prescribed, and keep taking it for the full period -- usually months after the blood count is normal, to refill the body''s stores.\nTake iron with vitamin C, such as a glass of calamansi or orange juice, which helps absorption.\nEat iron-rich food: liver, red meat, fish, egg, malunggay, kangkong, beans, and fortified cereals.\nHave the blood test repeated when asked.\nLet the cause be investigated; the anaemia is a sign of something, and treating only the iron misses it.\nExpect dark stools from iron -- that is normal and not bleeding.',
 'Do not take iron with coffee, tea, milk, calcium or antacids; they block absorption. Leave two hours.\nDo not stop the iron as soon as you feel better; the stores are not yet refilled.\nDo not take more than prescribed, and keep iron well away from children -- an overdose is dangerous.\nDo not ignore heavy periods or black stools as a cause.',
 'Chest pain, severe breathlessness, or fainting.\nA fast pounding heartbeat at rest.\nBlack tarry stool, blood in the stool, or vomiting blood.\nVery heavy menstrual bleeding soaking through protection hourly.\nFeeling increasingly weak despite treatment.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW()),

-- ---------------- bones, joints and muscles ----------------
('Osteoarthritis', 'M17', 'osteoarthritis,oa,degenerative joint disease,knee arthritis,primary osteoarthritis',
 'The smooth cartilage that cushions the ends of the bones in a joint has worn thin, so the joint becomes stiff and painful and the surfaces no longer glide easily.',
 'Years of use, plus age, extra weight, previous injury to the joint, heavy physical work, and family tendency. The knee and the hip carry the most load and are affected most.',
 'Joint pain that is worse with use and towards the end of the day, stiffness after resting that eases with movement, creaking or grating, swelling, and gradually less movement in the joint.',
 'Keep moving: regular gentle activity such as walking, cycling or swimming keeps the joint nourished and the muscles strong.\nDo the strengthening exercises your therapist gave you, especially for the thigh muscles if it is your knee.\nLose weight if you are carrying extra -- every kilo off takes several kilos of load off the knee.\nUse a warm compress for stiffness and a cold pack for a swollen flare.\nWear cushioned, supportive, flat footwear.\nUse a cane or walking aid on the opposite side if it was advised; it is not a defeat, it protects the joint.\nTake the pain relief as prescribed, and discuss injections or surgery if pain is limiting your life.',
 'Do not rest the joint completely for long periods; muscles weaken quickly and the pain gets worse.\nAvoid deep squatting, kneeling, and long stair climbing if those set it off.\nDo not take anti-inflammatory painkillers long term without review, especially with kidney, stomach or heart problems.\nDo not ignore a joint that suddenly becomes hot and swollen.\nDo not rely on glucosamine or herbal rubs in place of exercise and weight control.',
 'A joint that is suddenly hot, red, very swollen and painful, with fever -- this can be an infected joint and needs same-day care.\nA joint that gives way, locks, or cannot bear weight at all.\nSudden severe pain after a fall, or an obvious deformity.\nNumbness, tingling or weakness below the joint.\nAfter joint replacement: fever, a leaking or opening wound, calf pain and swelling, or sudden breathlessness.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW()),

('Low back pain', 'M54.5', 'low back pain,lbp,lumbago,back pain,mechanical back pain',
 'Pain in the lower back, most often from the muscles, ligaments and joints there rather than from anything structurally damaged. The great majority settles within a few weeks.',
 'Commonly lifting badly, a twist or strain, long periods sitting or standing in one position, weak core muscles, extra weight, or simply years of use. Stress and poor sleep make the pain feel worse.',
 'Aching or sharp pain across the lower back, stiffness, muscle spasm, and pain that may spread into the buttock or thigh. Movement is often limited in the first days.',
 'Keep moving and carry on with gentle normal activity; this heals faster than bed rest.\nWalk a little, often, rather than sitting for long stretches.\nUse a warm compress for comfort in the first days.\nTake the pain relief you were prescribed so that you can stay active.\nLearn to lift properly: bend the knees, keep the load close, do not twist while lifting.\nStrengthen your core and stretch your hamstrings once the acute pain settles.\nSet up your chair, desk and bed so your back is supported, and sleep on a firm mattress.',
 'Do not take to bed for days; more than a day or two of rest makes it worse and slower.\nDo not lift heavy things, or twist and lift, while it is painful.\nDo not have a forceful massage or manipulation while there is leg weakness or numbness.\nDo not keep taking anti-inflammatory painkillers for weeks without review.\nDo not ignore pain that is waking you at night or steadily worsening.',
 'Weakness in a leg or foot, or numbness that is spreading.\nNumbness around the groin, buttocks or inner thighs, or loss of control of urine or bowels -- this is an emergency.\nSevere pain after a fall or accident.\nFever with back pain, or back pain with weight loss or night sweats.\nPain that is constant, worse at night, and not eased by any position.\nA history of cancer, osteoporosis, or long-term steroid use with new back pain.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW()),

('Gout', 'M10', 'gout,gouty arthritis,hyperuricemia,uric acid,podagra',
 'Sharp crystals of uric acid have formed inside a joint, and the body''s reaction to them causes a sudden, intensely painful attack. Between attacks the joint can feel entirely normal.',
 'Uric acid builds up in the blood when the body makes too much or the kidneys clear too little. Red meat, organ meats, shellfish, beer and sweet drinks push it up; so do some medicines, kidney problems and family tendency.',
 'A joint, classically the big toe, becoming suddenly very painful, red, hot and swollen, often overnight, so tender that even a bedsheet hurts. Attacks last days to weeks.',
 'Take the medicine for the attack as soon as it starts, exactly as prescribed -- the earlier it is started, the shorter the attack.\nRest the joint and raise it; a cold pack helps.\nDrink plenty of water, two to three litres a day unless you were told to limit fluids.\nIf you were given a daily uric-acid-lowering medicine, take it every day for life, including during an attack unless told otherwise.\nHave your uric acid, kidneys, blood pressure and sugar checked regularly.\nLose weight slowly if you are carrying extra.',
 'Do not stop the daily uric-acid-lowering medicine when the pain goes -- that is exactly when it is working.\nAvoid beer and spirits, sweet drinks and fruit juice high in fructose.\nCut down on organ meats, red meat, sardines, shellfish and rich broths.\nDo not start the lowering medicine for the first time in the middle of an attack unless your doctor directed it.\nDo not crash diet or fast, which can set off an attack.\nDo not take aspirin for the pain.',
 'A hot swollen joint with fever and chills -- an infected joint looks like gout and needs same-day assessment.\nSeveral joints affected at once, with feeling very unwell.\nA rash, mouth ulcers or fever after starting a new gout medicine -- stop and call.\nSevere pain not responding to the medicine within a day or two.\nFlank pain or blood in the urine, which can mean a uric acid stone.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW()),

-- ---------------- head, ears, nose and throat ----------------
('Migraine', 'G43', 'migraine,migraine headache,migraine with aura,migraine without aura',
 'A migraine is not just a bad headache: it is a temporary change in how the brain and its blood vessels behave, which is why light, sound and movement all become unbearable and why it can bring nausea with it.',
 'The tendency is usually inherited. Attacks are set off by missed meals, poor or changed sleep, stress or the let-down after it, dehydration, bright or flickering light, strong smells, alcohol, certain foods, and hormonal changes around periods.',
 'Throbbing pain often on one side, worse with movement, with nausea or vomiting, and dislike of light, sound and smell. Some people see flashing lights or zigzags, or have tingling, for 20 to 60 minutes beforehand.',
 'Take your migraine medicine at the very first sign; waiting until the pain is established makes it much less effective.\nLie down in a dark quiet room and try to sleep.\nDrink water, and eat something if you have not.\nKeep a headache diary of attacks, what you had eaten, slept and been doing -- it is how triggers are actually found.\nKeep meals and sleep regular, including at weekends.\nTake the daily preventive medicine every day if you were given one; it takes weeks to show an effect.',
 'Do not use painkillers more than about two days a week over months -- that itself causes a constant headache that is hard to unpick.\nDo not skip meals or sleep.\nDo not wait to see whether the attack passes before taking your medicine.\nAvoid your own known triggers, and alcohol during an attack.',
 'The worst headache of your life, or one that comes on suddenly like a thunderclap.\nHeadache with fever and a stiff neck, or with a rash.\nHeadache with weakness, numbness, slurred speech, confusion, or trouble seeing that does not clear within an hour.\nA headache after a head injury, or one made much worse by coughing, straining or lying down.\nA new headache pattern after the age of 50, or one steadily worsening over weeks.\nA first migraine-like attack in pregnancy, or with a seizure.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW()),

('Vertigo', 'H81', 'vertigo,bppv,benign paroxysmal positional vertigo,labyrinthitis,vestibular neuritis,dizziness',
 'A false sense that you or the room is spinning, coming from the balance organ in the inner ear rather than from the brain. It is frightening but usually not dangerous, and it usually settles.',
 'Most often tiny crystals have moved into the wrong part of the inner ear, so head movement sends the wrong signal. It can also follow a viral infection of the balance nerve, or come with inner ear fluid problems or migraine.',
 'Spinning that comes on when you turn over in bed, lie down or look up, lasting seconds to minutes; with nausea, vomiting, unsteadiness, and sometimes ringing or fullness in one ear.',
 'Sit or lie down as soon as the spinning starts, and keep your eyes open and fixed on something still.\nGet up slowly and in stages, especially from bed.\nSleep slightly propped up for the first nights.\nAsk your doctor or therapist about the repositioning manoeuvre -- for the crystal type it often fixes it in one visit.\nDo the balance exercises you were given; the brain compensates faster with movement than with rest.\nMake the house safer: night light, clear floors, grab rail in the bathroom.\nDrink enough fluid and do not skip meals.',
 'Do not drive, ride a motorcycle, swim, or climb ladders or scaffolding while the attacks are happening.\nDo not stay completely still for days; some movement speeds recovery.\nDo not take motion sickness or sedating medicine for more than a few days unless advised -- it slows the brain''s adjustment.\nDo not make sudden sharp head movements in the early days.',
 'Vertigo with weakness or numbness, slurred speech, trouble swallowing, double vision or drooping face -- this needs emergency assessment.\nA severe headache, or a new unsteadiness so bad you cannot stand or walk at all.\nSudden hearing loss in one ear.\nVertigo after a head injury.\nConstant dizziness that does not come in episodes and is getting worse.\nChest pain, fainting, or an irregular heartbeat.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW()),

('Upper respiratory tract infection', 'J06', 'urti,upper respiratory tract infection,common cold,sipon,viral uri,nasopharyngitis',
 'An infection of the nose and throat -- the common cold. It is caused by a virus, so it clears on its own, and treatment is about being comfortable while it does.',
 'One of many viruses, passed on in coughs and sneezes and on hands and surfaces. Adults get several a year and children more.',
 'Blocked or runny nose, sneezing, sore throat, cough, mild headache, a mild fever, and feeling tired. It usually peaks at two to three days and clears in a week to ten days.',
 'Rest and drink plenty of fluids.\nTake paracetamol for fever, sore throat and aches as directed.\nGargle with warm salt water for the throat.\nUse steam inhalation or saline drops for a blocked nose, and saline drops for a blocked baby.\nWash hands often, cover coughs, and stay home while feverish.\nLet a lingering dry cough settle on its own; it can take two weeks after the rest has gone.',
 'Do not take antibiotics for a cold; they do not work on viruses and cause side effects and resistance.\nDo not give cough and cold medicines to children under six unless a doctor prescribed them.\nDo not give aspirin to children or teenagers.\nDo not smoke, which prolongs the cough.\nDo not use a decongestant nasal spray for more than three days; the blockage rebounds worse.',
 'Fever above 38 degrees for more than three days, or a fever that settles and then returns.\nBreathlessness, wheeze, chest pain, or breathing fast.\nSevere sore throat with drooling, or unable to swallow or open the mouth.\nEar pain, or face pain and swelling.\nA cough lasting more than three weeks, or coughing up blood.\nIn a baby: poor feeding, fewer wet nappies, floppiness, or noisy difficult breathing.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW()),

('Acute tonsillitis', 'J03', 'tonsillitis,acute tonsillitis,tonsilitis,sore throat,pharyngotonsillitis',
 'The tonsils at the back of the throat are infected and swollen. Most cases are viral; some are caused by a bacterium called streptococcus, which is the reason a throat swab or score is sometimes used before deciding on antibiotics.',
 'A virus, or streptococcal bacteria, passed on in coughs, sneezes and shared utensils. Commonest in children and young adults.',
 'Sore throat, pain on swallowing, swollen red tonsils sometimes with white patches, fever, tender glands in the neck, bad breath, and headache.',
 'Drink plenty of cool fluids, even though swallowing hurts -- staying hydrated matters more than what you drink.\nTake paracetamol for pain and fever as directed.\nGargle warm salt water, and try cold food like ice cream or gelatin for a child who will not drink.\nIf antibiotics were prescribed, finish the whole course.\nRest, and stay off school or work until the fever has gone for a day.\nUse a separate glass and utensils while ill.',
 'Do not insist on antibiotics for a viral sore throat.\nDo not stop a prescribed antibiotic course early -- for streptococcal throat the full course prevents complications affecting the heart and kidneys.\nDo not smoke, and avoid smoky air.\nDo not share drinks or utensils while ill.',
 'Difficulty breathing, noisy breathing, or drooling because swallowing is impossible.\nUnable to open the mouth fully, a voice that sounds muffled, or swelling on one side pushing the tonsil across.\nUnable to drink at all, or signs of drying out.\nFever above 38.5 degrees that is not settling after three days of antibiotics.\nA rash, dark urine, or puffy face and ankles in the weeks after a throat infection.\nNeck stiffness with severe headache.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW()),

('Allergic rhinitis', 'J30', 'allergic rhinitis,hay fever,nasal allergy,allergic rhinosinusitis,sinus allergy',
 'The lining of the nose reacts to something harmless in the air as though it were a threat, and swells and runs. It is an allergy, not an infection, which is why it has no fever and does not pass in a week.',
 'House dust mites, cockroach, mould, animal dander, pollen and strong smells. It runs in families and often goes with asthma and eczema.',
 'Sneezing in runs, clear runny nose, blocked nose, itchy nose, eyes and throat, and sometimes loss of smell, a dry cough or feeling tired from disturbed sleep.',
 'Use the steroid nasal spray every day as prescribed, aimed slightly outward, not daily-as-needed -- it takes a week or two to show full benefit.\nRinse the nose with saline to clear allergens and mucus.\nWash bedding weekly in hot water, and use covers on pillows and mattress if dust mite is the trigger.\nDamp-wipe and vacuum rather than dry-sweeping, and deal with cockroaches and damp patches.\nKeep windows closed on dusty or high-pollen days, and shower after being outdoors.\nTake the antihistamine you were prescribed, preferably a non-drowsy one in the daytime.',
 'Do not use a decongestant nasal spray for more than three days -- it rebounds and leaves the nose permanently blocked.\nDo not stop the steroid spray after two days because nothing has changed yet.\nDo not keep furry pets in the bedroom if animal dander is your trigger.\nDo not smoke, and keep smoke out of the house.\nDo not treat it as a cold with repeated antibiotics.',
 'Face pain or pressure with a fever, or thick green discharge for more than ten days -- this may be sinusitis.\nWheezing, breathlessness or a persistent night cough, which can mean asthma.\nNosebleeds that keep happening while using a nasal spray.\nA blockage on one side only, or blood-stained discharge on one side.\nSwelling around an eye, or changes in vision.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW()),

-- ---------------- skin ----------------
('Cellulitis', 'L03', 'cellulitis,skin infection,soft tissue infection,erysipelas',
 'A bacterial infection of the skin and the soft tissue beneath it. The area becomes red, hot and swollen, and the infection can spread quickly, which is why it is treated with antibiotics rather than left alone.',
 'Bacteria get in through a break in the skin: a cut, insect bite, athlete''s foot between the toes, an ulcer or a surgical wound. Diabetes, poor circulation, leg swelling and a weak immune system make it easier and more serious.',
 'An area of skin that is red, hot, swollen and tender, with a spreading edge; sometimes blisters, and fever and chills.',
 'Finish the entire course of antibiotics.\nRest and raise the affected part above heart level as much as you can -- this does as much as the tablets for the swelling.\nMark the edge of the redness with a pen and the date, so you and your doctor can see whether it is spreading or receding.\nTake paracetamol for pain and fever as directed.\nKeep the skin clean, and treat athlete''s foot between the toes if that was the way in.\nIf you have diabetes or leg swelling, check your skin daily and moisturise to prevent cracks.',
 'Do not stop antibiotics when the redness starts to fade.\nDo not walk about on an infected leg all day; elevation is part of the treatment.\nDo not apply a hot compress, and do not squeeze or try to drain any lump.\nDo not ignore spread beyond the pen mark.\nDo not treat it at home if you have diabetes -- the threshold for being seen is much lower.',
 'Redness spreading beyond the marked line, especially quickly.\nFever and chills, or feeling very unwell.\nSevere pain out of proportion to how the skin looks, skin turning dusky, purple or black, blisters, or a crackling feel under the skin -- emergency, immediately.\nRed streaks running up the limb, or swollen tender glands.\nNumbness, or the limb becoming cold or pale.\nNo improvement after two to three days of antibiotics.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW()),

('Eczema (dermatitis)', 'L30', 'eczema,dermatitis,atopic dermatitis,contact dermatitis,skin asthma',
 'The skin''s outer barrier does not hold moisture properly, so it dries, cracks and becomes inflamed and intensely itchy. The itch and the scratching keep it going, which is why breaking that cycle is the whole treatment.',
 'The tendency is inherited and often goes with asthma and allergic rhinitis. Flares come from soap and detergent, heat and sweat, dust, wool, stress, and sometimes certain foods.',
 'Dry, itchy, scaly patches, redness, oozing or crusting when scratched, and thickened darker skin in places scratched over a long time. In children it favours the cheeks, and the creases of the elbows and knees.',
 'Moisturise generously and often, at least twice a day and always within minutes of bathing, even when the skin looks good.\nUse the steroid or other prescribed cream on flares exactly as directed, in the amount and for the number of days given.\nBathe in lukewarm water, briefly, with a mild soap-free cleanser, and pat dry rather than rubbing.\nKeep nails short, and cover itchy patches at night if scratching in sleep is the problem.\nWear loose cotton clothing, and wash new clothes before wearing.\nIdentify and remove your own triggers; a diary helps.',
 'Do not use ordinary soap, bubble bath or antiseptic washes on eczema skin.\nDo not take long hot showers or baths.\nDo not stop moisturising once the flare settles -- that is what prevents the next one.\nDo not use a strong steroid cream on the face or in the folds unless it was prescribed for there, and do not use any of them continuously for weeks without review.\nDo not use someone else''s cream, or whitening and herbal creams of unknown content.\nDo not cut out major foods from a child''s diet without advice.',
 'Skin that is weeping, crusted yellow, or has pus-filled spots, with fever -- this is infected.\nPainful small blisters spreading in clusters, which can be a herpes infection on eczema and needs urgent treatment.\nA flare covering most of the body, with shivering or feeling unwell.\nEczema around the eyes with changes in vision.\nNo improvement after a week or two of correct treatment.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW()),

('Scabies', 'B86', 'scabies,galis,sarcoptes,itch mite',
 'A tiny mite has burrowed into the top layer of the skin. The intense itch is an allergic reaction to the mite and its waste, which is why it can take weeks to appear and weeks to settle even after successful treatment.',
 'Close and prolonged skin-to-skin contact, so it moves through households, sleeping spaces and dormitories. Occasionally through shared bedding and clothing. It is not a sign of being unclean.',
 'Intense itching, markedly worse at night; a rash of small bumps and thin wavy tracks, typically between the fingers, at the wrists, armpits, waistline, buttocks and genitals. In babies it also affects the palms, soles, face and scalp.',
 'Apply the prescribed cream or lotion over the whole body from the neck down -- including between the fingers and toes, under the nails, the navel, the buttocks and the genitals -- and in babies and the elderly, the face and scalp too.\nLeave it on for the full number of hours instructed, then wash it off; reapply if any part was washed during that time.\nTreat everybody in the household on the same day, even those with no itch at all.\nOn the day of treatment, wash all bedding, towels and recently worn clothes in hot water and dry in sunlight or a hot dryer.\nSeal anything that cannot be washed in a plastic bag for at least three days.\nExpect itching to continue for two to four weeks after successful treatment, and use the soothing cream or antihistamine you were given.\nRepeat the treatment after a week if you were told to.',
 'Do not treat only the person who itches; untreated family members reinfest everyone.\nDo not skip areas, and do not apply only to the rash.\nDo not wash the cream off early, or bathe just before applying it unless instructed.\nDo not keep reapplying the treatment because the itch continues -- that irritates the skin and is not needed.\nDo not scratch until it bleeds; broken skin gets infected.\nDo not share beds, towels or clothing until treatment is done.',
 'Skin becoming red, warm, swollen, crusted or oozing pus -- infected scratches.\nFever, or red streaks spreading from a scratched area.\nThick crusted scaly patches spreading widely, especially in an elderly or immune-weakened person; this form is far more contagious and needs urgent treatment.\nItch no better four weeks after treatment, or a new rash appearing.\nDark urine or puffy face in the weeks after an infected skin sore.',
 'Hospital clinical guidelines', 1, NOW());
